1. A standard split model and only one acquired coordinate bridge
----------------------------------------------------------------

Put H=M_2(k), bar(x)=tr(x) I-x, and n(x)=det(x).  Use the following fixed
Cayley--Dickson convention, including the order of its factors:

  C_0=H+H l,
  (x+y l)(z+w l)=(xz-bar(w)y)+(wx+y bar(z))l,
  N_0(x+y l)=det(x)+det(y),       l^2=-1.                (1)

It is the split Cayley algebra: it is a unital composition algebra and its
norm is isotropic already on H, since determinant has nonzero singular
vectors.  All later coordinate formulas refer to (1), not to an unspecified
octonion convention.

One actual Cayley frame in the acquired C suffices to identify C with C_0.
Choose pure i,u of norm1 with B(i,u)=0, and v perpendicular to
H_0=<1,i,u,iu>_k with N(v)=1.  The classical frame theorem says that

  1,i,u,iu,v,iv,uv,(iu)v                                  (2)

is a basis with multiplication determined by these norms and brackets.
Match it to a norm-one Cayley frame in (1).  The resulting basis map is
an algebra isomorphism.  Root must check the actual vectors, all indicated
products/orthogonalities, their norms using 7 beta, and the basis determinant.
No unknown historical octonion basis is required.

Existence here is effective and small in dimension, without a full-vector
enumeration.  In a nondegenerate quadratic complement of dimension>=2,
diagonalize and use two nonzero diagonal coordinates.  For s!=0 the two
sets {a x^2:x in k} and {s-b y^2:y in k} each have (q+1)/2 elements,
so intersect.  At most q^2 coefficient pairs suffice per chosen vector.
This is the already retained finite-form/frame argument, not an acquired
search, implementation or performance measurement.

2. The FULL rational centralizer of one involution
--------------------------------------------------

Let a be a nonidentity involution in Aut(C).  The retained eigencharacter
lemma gives dimensions4+4 on C.  Its fixed4-space H_a is a nondegenerate
unital composition subalgebra, hence a quaternion algebra.  Over a finite
field it is split, H_a isomorphic to M_2(k): its nondegenerate4-dimensional
norm has a nonzero isotropic vector by the elementary finite quadratic-form
argument, so the quaternion algebra has a zero divisor and is split.
Its negative space is H_a^perp.
Choose l_a in that complement of norm1.  Doubling gives H_a+H_a l_a with
the convention (1), so a becomes

  a_0(x+y l)=x-y l.

This also proves that all nonidentity involutions of Aut(C) are rationally
conjugate: identify two fixed quaternion algebras by a norm-preserving
isomorphism and extend it using their norm-one doubling vectors.  Such an
isomorphism exists because both quaternion algebras are M_2(k).

An automorphism commutes with a_0 if and only if it preserves H.  Indeed it
then preserves H^perp, hence both eigenspaces, and the converse is immediate.
Every element of K=C_G(a_0) has the following unique form up to the stated
scalar equivalence:

  rho_(p,q)(x+y l)=p x p^-1+(q y p^-1)l,
  p,q in GL_2(k), det(p)=det(q),
  (p,q)~(lambda p,lambda q), lambda in k^*.              (3)

Proof that (3) is an algebra automorphism.  Put d=det(p)=det(q).
The second component of the product of the two images is

  (q w p^-1)(p x p^-1)+(q y p^-1)(p bar(z) p^-1)
    =q(wx+y bar(z))p^-1.

In its first component use bar(p^-1)=p/d and bar(q)q=d I; the norm term is

  overline(q w p^-1)(q y p^-1)=p bar(w)y p^-1.

Both components therefore equal the image of (1).  These maps preserve
the norm and a_0 as well.

For completeness, an automorphism g stabilizing H acts there by p x p^-1:
every automorphism of M_2(k) is inner.  Write g(l)=u l.  Norm preservation
gives det(u)=1.  Since (x,0)(0,u)=(0,ux),

  g(y l)=g(y)g(l)=(u p y p^-1)l.

Thus q=u p and det(q)=det(p), proving surjectivity.  The kernel of (p,q)
acting on C is exactly the common scalar pairs.  Composition is the usual
paired multiplication, so

  K = {(p,q):det(p)=det(q)}/k^*,
  |K|=|GL_2(q)|^2/(q-1)^2=q^2(q^2-1)^2.               (4)

This is the full rational group, not just the central product
(SL_2(q) x SL_2(q))/<(-I,-I)>.  The latter is the subgroup with square
determinant class in (3), of index2.  Dropping the nonsquare pairs would
lose actual involutions and invalidate completeness.  The additional coset
does not come from a field or other outer automorphism; it already lies
in the rational quaternion stabilizer inside G.

The quaternion-stabilizer description is classical.  Wilson's retained
quaternion/frame facts and the elementary argument above suffice here.
As an external primary cross-check, Dijols, MPIM22-43, Lemma5.1 and its
proof describe the quaternion stabilizer as SO4 and the inner quaternion
action.  We do not use that paper's p-adic representation results or its
code, nor infer finite rational surjectivity merely from an algebraic-group
dimension count; (3) supplies the rational points directly.

3. One complete involution catalogue, with both determinant classes
------------------------------------------------------------------

Squaring (3) shows that rho_(p,q) is an involution precisely when

  p^2=q^2=lambda I for one lambda!=0.                    (5)

By Cayley--Hamilton, a nonscalar2-by-2 matrix with scalar square has trace0.
If p=alpha I is scalar, det(q)=alpha^2.  A nonscalar trace-zero q would
have q^2=-alpha^2 I, contrary to (5) in odd characteristic.  Thus q is
scalar too, and the two possibilities are the identity and a_0.
Otherwise both p and q are trace zero; their common nonzero determinant d
then gives p^2=q^2=-d I, so every such pair is an involution.

Fix a nonsquare eta.  Scaling both matrices in a pair reduces d to exactly
one delta in {1,eta}.  The only residual scalar equivalence is simultaneous
negation.  Hence the following is a COMPLETE, DUPLICATE-FREE parametrization:

  J={a_0} disjoint-union
    union_(delta in {1,eta})
    { (p,q):tr(p)=tr(q)=0, det(p)=det(q)=delta }/{+1,-1}.
                                                               (6)

Choose one of (p,q),(-p,-q) by a fixed literal coefficient ordering.  This
quotient is free: the matrices are invertible and the characteristic is odd.
No generic subgroup-centralizer or conjugacy-class calculation proves the
completeness of (6); it follows from (3)--(5).

For a trace-zero p=[[x,y],[z,-x]], its determinant condition is

  x^2+yz=-delta.

Write epsilon_delta=quadratic_character(-delta).  There are
1+epsilon_delta roots x with x^2=-delta.  For those x, yz=0 has2q-1
solutions, and for every other x it has q-1.  Thus

  n_delta=#p=q(q+epsilon_delta),
  |J|=1+(n_1^2+n_eta^2)/2=q^4+q^2+1.                   (7)

For q11 this is14763.  This is an abstract centralizer-domain count, not
a new polytope/candidate census or an acquired list of coordinates.
Retain each catalogue entry as its delta and two trace-zero triples; a
7-by-7 matrix need only be constructed when used.  There is no reason to
store14763 full177156-point permutations.

For completeness about the old pool types, the two delta parts of (6)
are each single K-conjugacy classes.  Two trace-zero matrices with the
same nonzero determinant are GL_2-conjugate, since their common polynomial
X^2+delta is separable.  Choose conjugators for p and q separately.  Their
determinants can be made equal by multiplying one by an element of the
quadratic centralizer: its determinant is the norm of a split quadratic
algebra or F_(q^2), and this norm is surjective over a finite field.  The
adjusted pair lies in (3).  The three involution classes in K are therefore
{a_0} and the two delta parts; the noncentral class centralizers have orders

  |K|/(n_delta^2/2)=2(q-epsilon_delta)^2.                (8)

4. Exactly two ordered commuting-endpoint types, and small full stabilizers
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fix an ordered pair of distinct commuting involutions (a,e).  First send
